## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Analysis

Your code has **three** Thread.Abort patterns:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| **ThreadAbortException catch** | `catch` block with rollback | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| **Thread.ResetAbort** | Prevents rethrow for clean `finally` | Remove — `OperationCanceledException` doesn't auto-rethrow |
| **Blocking call interruption** | `GetNextItem()` may block | Pass token to unblock |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — replaces ThreadAbortException catch
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — OperationCanceledException doesn't
                // auto-rethrow, so finally runs cleanly and the loop exits
                // via the while condition.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after Join, the thread is truly stuck in
        // uncooperative code. There is NO equivalent to Thread.Abort
        // in .NET 8. See pitfall #3 below.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### Key Changes You Must Also Make

**`GetNextItem` must accept and respect the token.** If it blocks (e.g., on a queue), the token is the only way to wake it:

```csharp
// BEFORE — blocks forever, only Thread.Abort could interrupt
private Item GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks until item available
}

// AFTER — unblocks when cancelled
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException
}
```

If it uses `Thread.Sleep`, replace with:
```csharp
// BEFORE
Thread.Sleep(1000);

// AFTER — wakes immediately on cancellation
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(1000);
```

### Critical Pitfalls

| # | Pitfall | Why It Matters |
|---|---------|---------------|
| 1 | **Not passing the token to `GetNextItem`** | If `GetNextItem` blocks without checking the token, `Cancel()` has no effect. The thread stays stuck. This is the #1 cause of "cancellation doesn't work." |
| 2 | **Using `Thread.Sleep(2000)` instead of `Join`** | Your original `Stop()` used `Sleep` — a race condition. `Join(timeout)` actually waits for the thread to exit. |
| 3 | **No fallback for truly stuck threads** | .NET 8 has **no** `Thread.Abort`. If code can't be modified to check the token (e.g., a native call), you must move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`. |
| 4 | **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | `CTS` is `IDisposable`. Leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. Always dispose via `using` or `IDisposable` pattern. |
| 5 | **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to only catch *your* cancellation, not an unrelated one from inner code. |
| 6 | **Assuming cancellation is instant** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at the next checkpoint. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it does long-running work. |

### Verification Checklist

- [ ] No `Thread.Abort`, `ThreadAbortException`, or `ResetAbort` remain
- [ ] Every blocking/long-running method in the chain accepts `CancellationToken`
- [ ] `CancellationTokenSource` is disposed
- [ ] Builds with zero `SYSLIB0006` warnings on `net8.0`
